PRSENSE(1)
PRSENSE — principled AI-assisted pull request review

⸻

NAME
prsense — CLI-first, self-hosted AI code reviewer

⸻

SYNOPSIS
prsense  [options] [target]

⸻

DESCRIPTION
PRsense is a command-line tool for reviewing code changes using large
language models with full repository context.

It is designed for senior developers who want intentional, inspectable,
and self-hosted reviews without relying on hosted SaaS platforms.

PRsense operates primarily on diffs and surrounding context, producing
structured review signals that prioritize clarity, correctness, and
maintainability.


⸻

COMMANDS

review      Review a code change and emit structured review signals
index       Index a repository for faster and deeper contextual reviews
doctor      Diagnose PRsense installation and runtime environment
help        Show help for a command


⸻

COMMAND: review

NAME
prsense review — review a code change

SYNOPSIS
prsense review [target] [options]

DESCRIPTION
Reviews a code change and produces structured review signals.

The review analyzes:
- the diff itself
- surrounding code context
- repository conventions
- test and documentation impact

The default output is human-readable text suitable for terminal usage.
Machine-readable JSON output is also supported.

TARGETS
The review target may be one of:

.                       Current repository (default)
<path>                  Path to a local repository
<git-range>             Git revision range (e.g. origin/main..HEAD)
<patch-file>            Unified diff file
-                       Read diff from stdin

OPTIONS
–base-branch
Override the base branch used for diff comparison.

--max-signals <n>
    Maximum number of review signals to emit.

--confidence <0-1>
    Minimum confidence required for a signal to be reported.

--context <n>
    Maximum number of context chunks retrieved during review.

--no-context
    Perform a diff-only review without contextual retrieval.

--explain
    Include reasoning details and context references in output.

--format <text|json>
    Output format. Default is text.

EXAMPLES
prsense review .
prsense review origin/main..HEAD
prsense review diff.patch
git diff | prsense review -
prsense review . –confidence 0.7 –max-signals 5

⸻

COMMAND: index

NAME
prsense index — index repository for contextual review

SYNOPSIS
prsense index [path] [options]

DESCRIPTION
Indexes a repository for contextual retrieval (RAG).

Indexing enables PRsense to retrieve relevant surrounding code during
reviews. The process is idempotent and safe to run multiple times.

Indexing is not required for basic reviews, but improves depth and quality.

TARGETS
                  Path to a local repository (default: current directory)

OPTIONS
–force
Rebuild the index from scratch.

--stats
    Print indexing statistics after completion.

--dry-run
    Show what would be indexed without performing writes.

EXAMPLES
prsense index .
prsense index ../my-repo –force
prsense index . –stats

⸻

COMMAND: doctor

NAME
prsense doctor — diagnose environment and configuration

SYNOPSIS
prsense doctor

DESCRIPTION
Performs a series of checks to validate that PRsense is correctly
installed and configured.

This command is intended to help diagnose missing dependencies,
misconfiguration, or runtime issues.

CHECKS PERFORMED
- Environment variables
- LLM provider availability
- Vector database connectivity
- Config file discovery
- Filesystem access
- Version compatibility

OUTPUT
The output lists checks with clear pass/fail status and actionable
remediation steps.

EXAMPLES
prsense doctor

⸻

CONFIGURATION

PRsense uses two kinds of configuration:

1. User configuration (review behavior)
2. Environment variables (runtime and infrastructure)

USER CONFIGURATION
A file named prsense.yml may be placed at the repository root.

Example:

    llm:
      provider: ollama
      model: qwen2.5-coder
      temperature: 0.1

    review:
      confidenceThreshold: 0.6
      maxSignals: 10

    context:
      maxChunks: 5

    git:
      baseBranch: trunk

If no configuration file is found, PRsense uses built-in defaults.

ENVIRONMENT VARIABLES
Environment variables configure runtime and infrastructure details.

Common variables include:

    PRSENSE_OPENAI_API_KEY
    PRSENSE_OLLAMA_HOST
    PRSENSE_DATABASE_URL
    PRSENSE_LOG_LEVEL

Environment variables do not control review behavior.

⸻

EXIT STATUS

0   Successful execution
1   Invalid usage or configuration
2   Runtime or dependency failure
3   Review completed with internal errors


⸻

FILES

prsense.yml
    Repository-local review configuration.

~/.config/prsense/config.yml
    Optional user-level configuration (fallback).


⸻

DESIGN PHILOSOPHY

PRsense is intentionally:

- CLI-first
- Self-hosted
- Transparent
- Opinionated but configurable
- Focused on judgment, not enforcement

It favors clarity and trust over automation and noise.


